<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Formigny</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1450">1450</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Formigny was fought on 15 April 1450 in Normandy, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. A French army under the Count of Clermont, joined by Breton troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-iii-duke-of-brittany">Arthur de Richemont</a>, destroyed an English relief force under Sir Thomas Kyriell. The victory led to the French reconquest of Normandy, completed with the fall of Cherbourg in August 1450.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-formigny.jpg" alt="Battle of Formigny" width="320" height="295" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Formigny.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vii-of-france">Charles VII of France</a> used the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> of 1444 to reorganise his armies, while the English, divided by the struggle for power that would lead to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>, left their forces in Normandy scattered and weak. The French broke the truce in June 1449 and by October occupied much of Normandy; the Bureau brothers took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> in October, Harfleur in December and Honfleur in January 1450 before investing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>. An English expeditionary force assembled at Portsmouth under Sir Thomas Kyriell, a relatively unknown commander.</p><p>Lack of money lowered morale, and in January 1450 the troops lynched an official sent to negotiate their pay. Kyriell landed at Cherbourg on 15 March with some 2,500 men, joined by another 1,800 collected by the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Rather than relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>, Kyriell took Valognes on 27 March and marched on Carentan, which he reached on 12 April. The French commander, the Count of Clermont, refused battle, so Kyriell marched toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> and halted near Formigny on 14 April, followed by 3,000 men under Clermont. Arthur de Richemont, Constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, reached Saint-Lo the same day with a Breton force of 2,000.</p><p>On 15 April the armies faced each other on the Carentan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> road. The English, under 4,000 men of whom 2,900 were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, stood with their backs to a stream and had no time to make the usual defence of sharpened stakes. Clermont's dismounted men-at-arms attacked and were repulsed, as were two cavalry probes; he then had two culverins fire from beyond bowshot, and the archers left their positions to try to seize them. Around 7 p.m. Richemont's 1,200 cavalry arrived from the south.</p><p>Caught in the open by the Breton cavalry and enveloped by Clermont and Pierre de Breze, the English were broken into small groups, and most were killed or captured, Kyriell among them. A French writer claimed that about 500 captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> were massacred afterwards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Kyriell's army was effectively destroyed, the last significant English field army in Normandy. French records claim 3,744 bodies were buried, though that count seems to include both sides, and put the prisoners at 1,200 to 1,400; other historians suggest 2,500 killed and 900 captured. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> fell on 12 June and Cherbourg on 12 August. Formigny is notable as one of the first recorded uses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">field artillery</a> on a European battlefield, though accounts are unclear how effective the guns were; the noise of their firing alerted Richemont to the battle and brought him onto the field.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Formigny” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Arthur de Richemont, constable of France and later Duke Arthur III, helped reform the French royal army and shared in the victory at the <b>Battle of Formigny</b> in 1450.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Recovery came with Joan of Arc's relief of Orléans in 1429 and with Charles VII's reforms, which in 1445 created the compagnies d'ordonnance, the first permanent royal cavalry, and used artillery to win at <b>Formigny</b> in 1450 and Castillon in 1453.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Military</a></li></ul></section>
